{"id": "98e92d98-55f8-4d24-87d2-10e26f1cb4c0", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T00:11:08.787322", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "8301f324-f56b-42bf-abe5-b365349c954d", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Environment inspected. Confirming scope before any design drafting."}], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: bare environment. Only /workspace/task.json. No ML stack, no GPU driver access, no network, no datasets, no attack harnesses.\n\nNow produce the scoping pass at plan stage: inspection findings + outline approach + assumptions/uncertainties + non-identifiability flags.\n\nI should reference the researcher_topics: neurips2024/97431/11:setup_17 and setup_18 — these look like NeurIPS 2024 paper 97431 setup topics; I don't have access to those documents. Only track.md is in task.json documents. I should flag that I cannot see the source documents for the topics (only track.md), so setup details referenced by those topics are assumptions to be resolved by the researcher.\n\nNow the substantive design outline. Let me think carefully about the science.\n\nQuestion: how do combined jailbreak techniques — attacker strength, defense strength, previously tested tricks — affect ASR under token-level (AutoDAN) and prompt-level (PAIR) attacks; interaction dynamics between attacker power and defense robustness across datasets and attack styles.\n\nFactor set and operationalization:\n\nA) Attack style (between, 2 levels, confounded with algorithm family): AutoDAN (token-level genetic + prefix optimization producing natural-language-looking adversarial suffix/prefix with fluency constraints) vs PAIR (prompt-level iterative black-box attacker-LLM template refinement, target-query exchanges, typically ≤20 iterations, N attacker templates). Note: attack style is a \"crossed\" factor but is fundamentally confounded with the algorithm instance (unless multiple algorithms per level — recommend treating style as a fixed effect but also allowing multiple algorithm variants per style if possible, otherwise \"style\" = algorithm family effect, unreplicable at level-1).\n\nB) Attacker strength (within-algorithm ordinal, 3-4 levels): query/compute budget. Operationalize as: \n   - AutoDAN: number of generations/iterations (e.g., G×T: population × generations), plus optional white-box access level (grey-box: logits/loss available for prefix optimization vs. black-box only scoring by judge). Actually AutoDAN is black-box by design (uses GA over natural-language prompt library + fluency/prefix constraints). So attacker strength for AutoDAN = number of generations/offspring, number of initial prompt templates, and whether the human-written \"liberal\" prompt library is the full released library or a reduced set.\n   - PAIR: number of attacker templates N and max iterations M (queries = N×M), plus attacker model capability (weak attacker e.g. 7B vs strong attacker e.g. 33B/GPT-4-class), and memory depth (number of previous attempts kept in context).\n   Unify as \"query budget\" (total target-model queries per harmful behavior) with matched levels {low, medium, high} e.g. ~10/50/200 queries per behavior, so budget is comparable across styles. Keep the \"within-style implementation knobs\" as nested sub-parameters so we don't claim the budget manipulation is identical across styles.\n   - Optional access factor: white-box (logits / token-level greedy like GCG) vs black-box (query-only). Careful: AutoDAN is black-box; adding a white-box access factor crossed with style makes \"token-level + white-box\" a third thing (GCG) — risk of a construct mess. Flag as identifiability issue: token-level vs prompt-level is operationalized by optimizer granularity, whereas access (white/black-box) is orthogonal but AutoDAN-as-published doesn't have a white-box variant; so recommend either dropping access or adding GCG as a third style level (which breaks the 2-level \"style\" contrast and changes compute profile).\n\nC) Defense strength (ordinal tiers, 4-5 levels):\n   D0 None (raw instruct model)\n   D1 System-prompt hardening / refined safety prompt (e.g. NeurIPS-tier \"self-reminder\" / refined safety prompt from the XSTest-style literature)\n   D2 Input-side filtering: perplexity + keyword/cipher detection filter (PPL filter as in AutoDAN's own evaluation; detection of obfuscated tokens) and output-side classifier moderation (e.g. Llama Guard class moderation)\n   D3 Alignment baseline = RLHF/instruct-tuned model as-is (the shipped model already alignment-trained) — careful: D0..D2 are applied on top of the aligned model, so \"RLHF baseline\" is D0. Better structure: two sub-factors: (i) alignment strength of the base: RLHF'd instruct vs strengthened alignment (e.g. extra R2D/ safety fine-tuning / strong-system-prompt + safety SFT), and (ii) run-time defenses: none / system prompt / PPL+keyword filter / output moderation. Making these two separate factors avoids the ordinal-tier confound where tier k bundles multiple mechanisms with different attack-specific vulnerabilities (perplexity filter hits token-level attacks specifically, which is exactly the interaction of interest but is destroyed if bundled into one ordinal tier).\n   Key point: a single ordinal \"defense strength\" ladder is not interpretable for the interaction question, because filtering defenses are style-specific (PPL filter disproportionately kills AutoDAN's token-level perturbations but not PAIR's semantic prompts). Recommend a mechanistic defense coding: {none, system-prompt, input-filter(PPL/keyword), output-moderation, strengthened-alignment} as crossed/nested rather than one ordinal axis, with the ordinal reading done post hoc via a defense-efficacy scale.\n\nD) Previously tested tricks exposure (2 levels, and this is the one most likely under-specified):\n   Operationalization: holdout structure over attack \"seeds\"/transformations.\n   - \"Previously publicized\" = transformations present in published attack libraries at time T0 (released AutoDAN prompt library, public GCG suffixes, published PAIR templates, public jailbreak-template corpora e.g. \"jailbreakchat\"-style template lists, and the fixed-adversarial-string family). These are \"seen\" and could already be baked into training data / defense fine-tuning data.\n   - \"Novel\" = transformations generated in-study by the optimizer (fresh AutoDAN GA runs, fresh PAIR templates) or a curated set held out from anything published before the target model cutoff, i.e. post-cutoff.\n   - Critical confound: novelty of transformation is confounded with the target-model training-data cutoff, and confounded with whether the attack is optimizer-generated (adaptive) vs static template (non-adaptive). Need to separate: (i) template provenance (public vs novel), and (ii) adaptivity (static prompt applied as-is vs optimizer adapting to target responses). Two factors, not one.\n   Also: the exposure factor interacts with whether the defense was fine-tuned on public attack strings (defense \"seen tricks\" side) — so exposure has attacker-side and defender-side versions. Cleanest: cross \"attack library exposure (novel/public)\" with \"defense exposure (defense fine-tuning data contains the public attack family: yes/no)\". If we can't control defense fine-tuning data (shipped models), then defense exposure is unmeasured → flag.\n\nDatasets: two senses — (a) harm/benchmark query sets (AdvBench split of 520 behaviors, TDC23 100 prompts, HarmBench, SORRY-Bench, OR-Bench, MWJ), (b) \"domain/topic splits\" within benchmark. Ask researcher. Recommend treating benchmark as a random/nested blocking factor with behaviors as the unit, and topic/domain (illegal, violence, self-harm, hate, privacy, fraud...) as a stratification covariate, because ASR varies wildly by topic and topic mix differs across benchmarks.\n\nUnit of analysis: the (behavior × attack-run) pair = a single attack attempt with a binary success outcome. Design: behaviors are crossed with everything (every behavior is attacked under every cell in principle), so behaviors are the sampling unit for generalization; runs/attacks are nested within behavior × cell. Random effects: behavior (intercept, maybe slope for defense), target model, attack seed.\n\nASR denominator: must be fixed and reported per level — key decisions:\n  - Denominator = all attempted behaviors (intent-level ASR per behavior, \"any of K attempts succeeds\" = attack success at the behavior level) vs per-attempt ASR (per query). Report both; primary = behavior-level ASR@budget with fixed total query budget, because per-attempt ASR mechanically inflates with budget (ASR grows monotonically with queries), so cross-style comparison at unequal query counts is invalid. This is the single most important scoring decision.\n  - Censoring/exclusions: exclude behaviors where the model is already safe-refusal-free? No. Exclude only behaviors the model answers harmlessly pre-attack (a \"pre-attack utility/refusal screen\"): if model already complies with the raw prompt, the cell contributes no headroom → treat as an eligibility screen, report screen-out rate per benchmark/topic, and keep it identical across conditions (screen is computed once, per model-benchmark, defense-free). Otherwise differential availability biases ASR.\n  - Scoring: judge ensemble (3 judges: GPT-4-class LLM judge with the AutoDAN/HarmBench-style scoring prompt; an open judge (e.g. Llama-Guard-3 / ShieldGemma classifier); and a rules/refusal-detector using refusal-string matching + keyword list) with majority vote, and report inter-judge agreement. Bias w.r.t. attack style: AutoDAN outputs often contain gibberish-adjacent text or an attack-prefix that leaks into the answer, so the judge prompt must be shown the response only, never the attack prompt, and judges must be validated on a manually-labeled 200–400 item audit sample balanced across style (stratified over style × defense × response-length decile) to estimate differential misclassification (style × judge interaction). Also report refusal-detection separately from harmful-compliance so that \"filter-caused refusals\" are distinguishable from \"genuine refusal to harmful content\" and from \"incoherent output treated as refusal\" — this matters because AutoDAN+PPL-filter produces refusals/truncations, and naive judges over-count those as failures of attack (which is the real effect) or under-count (which is bias). Add an output-coherence/control-condition check to separate \"attack destroyed capability\" from \"defense held.\"\n  - Also add a utility/harmlessness check: same-judge scoring on benign control prompts (e.g. XOR / benign AdvBench-subset) run through every defense tier — to quantify defense over-refusal, which is a necessary denominator correction for interpreting ASR differences.\n\nDesign type: fully crossed is 2 (style) × 3 (budget) × ~4 (defense) × 2 (exposure) × 2 (benchmark) × B behaviors × seeds — the compute explosion is driven by style because PAIR costs N×M target queries + attacker-model queries per behavior, whereas AutoDAN costs its own generations. Recommend: not fully crossed on all factors.\nFavor: split-plot / blocked factorial with style between-subjects (whole-plot = attack algorithm family, since style can't be manipulated within a single query), budget nested within style (each style has its own natural budget ladder, normalized to shared target-query budget levels), defense within-behavior (each behavior serves as its own block across defense tiers — cheap and high power), exposure within-behavior (each behavior attacked with public and novel library variants). Then:\n  - Use a blocked crossed design over the \"cheap\" in-run factors (defense × exposure within behavior) and a partially crossed design over the expensive factors: fully cross style × budget × defense × exposure × benchmark for a subset, drop the highest-order interactions with low prior via a fraction (e.g. run exposure × budget crossed only in one budget level, i.e. omit 3-way exposure×budget×defense for one budget level). Recommend explicit \"omitted highest-order interaction\" fractional scheme rather than random fractionalization, so the design's alias structure is legible.\n  - Power/sample: behaviors per benchmark ~100-200 (AdvBench 520 available; TDC23 100; SORRY-Bench 440), 3 seeds per (behavior × cell) for stochastic attacks (AutoDAN GA is stochastic; PAIR is stochastic via attacker sampling). Power for interactions: because outcome is binary and ASR at mid-range cells is ~0.3-0.7, use simulation-based power (pre-register a simulation with the mixed model, ICC from a pilot) rather than closed-form; target ≥100 behaviors × 3 seeds to detect an interaction OR ≈ 0.5-0.6 on the log-odds scale at 80% power with model-level variance.\n  - Normalize compute: report and cap per-attack target-query counts; equalized-budget cells are the basis of the headline comparison, and the full budget curve is the secondary analysis.\n\nAnalysis plan:\n  - Primary: logistic mixed-effects model (Bernoulli outcome, logit link) on the pooled attempt-level data: success ~ style * budget * defense_mechanism * exposure + topic + benchmark + (1 + budget|behavior) + (1|model) + (1|seed). Because style is between-subjects and perfectly collinear with algorithm instance, style main effects and style interactions are identified only up to algorithm-family sampling error → \"style × defense\" interaction is confounded with \"which specific algorithm implementation\" unless ≥2 algorithm variants per style are run (recommend a third PAIR-like (e.g., TAP) and a third AutoDAN-like (GAP/TAP/ AutoDAN variant) algorithm so that style is treated as a random sample of algorithms within style → then style × defense is a real cross-level interaction with algorithm-as-random-effect. That's a consequential design choice worth flagging: it doubles compute but is the only way to make \"attack style\" a generalizable factor rather than an idiosyncratic two-algorithm comparison.\n  - Also: budget is on the log scale; model ASR vs budget as a growth curve (e.g. ASR(q) saturating) — this reframes interaction dynamics as shifts in the budget-ASR curve (defense raises the query budget needed to reach criterion ASR, e.g. \"budget-to-50%-ASR\" or AUC-over-budget, an effect-size-friendly scalar). This is a nice answer to \"interaction dynamics\" that avoids the trap that any ASR comparison is budget-dependent.\n  - Report marginal predicted ASR with 95% CI per cell, interaction contrasts (ratio of odds ratios for style × defense with bootstrap CIs), effect sizes on both log-odds and risk-difference scales, and per-benchmark/topic stratified fits with random slopes; test cross-benchmark heterogeneity (I²/Q) to answer \"across datasets.\"\n  - Multiple comparisons: pre-register the interaction family (a small set of planned contrasts) + FDR for exploratory.\n  - Non-identifiability flags:\n    1. attack style ≡ algorithm instance (2 algorithms) → style interactions ungeneralizable unless algorithms nested within style with ≥2 per style.\n    2. attacker strength ≡ query budget ≠ optimizer quality; AutoDAN's strength knob (generations) is not commensurable with PAIR's (iterations × attacker capability) — normalizing by target queries hides the attacker-model cost asymmetry (PAIR's attacker LLM cost). Also PAIR's attack strength depends on the attacker model's own safety posture.\n    3. exposure (novel vs public) is confounded with model training cutoff and with defense training data (unmeasured for closed/shipped models) → cannot separate \"model memorized public tricks\" from \"public templates are weaker/stronger\". Needs a same-provenance contrast: hold provenance fixed and vary only public/private status within a single generated library (split optimizer outputs by similarity to a public library via embedding/MinHash matching).\n    4. Defense tiers bundling mechanisms → style × defense-tier interaction uninterpretable; must decompose.\n    5. Adaptivity: \"previously tested tricks\" also implies a defender who has tested/adapted. If the design includes a reactive defense (fine-tuned on observed attacks), that creates a dynamic game not a static factorial; recommend fixing defense non-adaptive for the primary and treating adaptive defense as a separate exploratory arm.\n    6. Target-model sampling: with 2-3 models, model effects are fixed but generalization is nil; model × defense interaction is confounded with alignment-provenance.\n    7. ASR denominator changes with the pre-attack eligibility screen and with judge → \"defense × judge\" sensitivity analysis required.\n\nCompute realism: state that with no GPU and no network here, nothing can be run; the design must be specified for external compute. Rough budget estimate: e.g. 2 styles × 3 budgets × 5 defenses × 2 exposures × 200 behaviors × 3 seeds attempts = but attempts aren't queries. PAIR: 200 behaviors × (3 budgets avg 60 queries) etc. Let me give an order-of-magnitude: primary grid ≈ 1.2e5 attack attempts; with PAIR mean ~30 target queries + 30 attacker calls per attempt and AutoDAN ~50-200 generations, total target-model forwards ≈ 1e7 → on a 7-8B model at ~50 tok/s/A100... this is big; I'll state a compute-normalized reduced grid and give numbers. Better: state the design should fix a target total of ~1e6–1e7 model generations and derive behavior counts accordingly; and propose a \"compute-matched\" subset.\n\nI'll present the numbers but mark them as planning estimates, not measurements.\n\nAlso flag: environment has no network → cannot fetch AutoDAN/PAIR repos, HF models, or API judges; design-only per track.md.\n\nNow let me draft the end_researcher_turn with stage=plan. Needs to be thorough but organized. Also list assumptions and uncertainties, and non-identifiable factors. Also mention researcher_topics documents (setup_17, setup_18) not present in this environment — I only have track.md, so those topic details must be supplied.\n\nBudget: 6 questions available. I could list the top clarifying questions. Let me include ~6 targeted questions.\n\nLet me write it.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "echo \"=== any docs besides task.json? ===\"; find / -xdev -maxdepth 4 \\( -iname \"track.md\" -o -iname \"setup_1*\" -o -iname \"*neurips*\" \\) 2>/dev/null | head; echo \"=== git repos ===\"; find / -xdev -maxdepth 4 -name \".git\" -type d 2>/dev/null | head; echo \"=== net check ===\"; timeout 5 python3 -c \"import urllib.request;print(urllib.request.urlopen('https://huggingface.co',timeout=4).status)\" 2>&1 | tail -2", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-80677672e9059ff0", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-80677672e9059ff0", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"echo \\\"=== any docs besides task.json? ===\\\"; find / -xdev -maxdepth 4 \\\\( -iname \\\"track.md\\\" -o -iname \\\"setup_1*\\\" -o -iname \\\"*neurips*\\\" \\\\) 2>/dev/null | head; echo \\\"=== git repos ===\\\"; find / -xdev -maxdepth 4 -name \\\".git\\\" -type d 2>/dev/null | head; echo \\\"=== net check ===\\\"; timeout 5 python3 -c \\\"import urllib.request;print(urllib.request.urlopen('https://huggingface.co',timeout=4).status)\\\" 2>&1 | tail -2\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-153bfe8e-db62-4aad-bae4-702a8118fefd", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "execute_check: {\"command\": \"echo \\\"=== any docs besides task.json? ===\\\"; find / -xdev -maxdepth 4 \\\\( -iname \\\"track.md\\\" -o -iname \\\"setup_1*\\\" -o -iname \\\"*neurips*\\\" \\\\) 2>/dev/null | head; echo \\\"=== git repos ===\\\"; find / -xdev -maxdepth 4 -name \\\".git\\\" -type d 2>/dev/null | head; echo \\\"=== net check ===\\\"; timeout 5 python3 -c \\\"import urllib.request;print(urllib.request.urlopen('https://huggingface.co',timeout=4).status)\\\" 2>&1 | tail -2\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
{"id": "cd8cfdd1-ed6d-4cdb-9716-6231c459b1f7", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T00:11:08.958666", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "98e92d98-55f8-4d24-87d2-10e26f1cb4c0", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-80677672e9059ff0", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"echo \\\"=== any docs besides task.json? ===\\\"; find / -xdev -maxdepth 4 \\\\( -iname \\\"track.md\\\" -o -iname \\\"setup_1*\\\" -o -iname \\\"*neurips*\\\" \\\\) 2>/dev/null | head; echo \\\"=== git repos ===\\\"; find / -xdev -maxdepth 4 -name \\\".git\\\" -type d 2>/dev/null | head; echo \\\"=== net check ===\\\"; timeout 5 python3 -c \\\"import urllib.request;print(urllib.request.urlopen('https://huggingface.co',timeout=4).status)\\\" 2>&1 | tail -2\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.165, \"output\": \"=== any docs besides task.json? ===\\n=== git repos ===\\n=== net check ===\\n    raise URLError(err)\\nurllib.error.URLError: <urlopen error [Errno -3] Temporary failure in name resolution>\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "98e92d98-55f8-4d24-87d2-10e26f1cb4c0", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "cb4a7878-575b-4cef-9c4a-dfd4dec1b233", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T00:15:58.959449", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "cd8cfdd1-ed6d-4cdb-9716-6231c459b1f7", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Environment fully inspected. Here is my scoping pass."}], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: no network, no repos, no docs other than task.json inline track.md note. Now produce the plan handoff.\n\nKeep the outline at the level requested: factor set + operationalization, design type, unit of analysis + ASR denominator/scoring, analysis plan + identifiability, assumptions, and unidentifiable flags.\n\nLet me think carefully about the substance to make it high-quality.\n\nFactors:\n1. Attack style (between-subjects, structural): token-level AutoDAN (genetic algorithm over natural-language \"human-readable\" adversarial suffix/prefix, fluency+perplexity-constrained, white-box-free/black-box) vs prompt-level PAIR (attacker LLM iteratively refines semantic jailbreak prompts, target-model queries). Key: these are not interchangeable levels of one factor; they differ in compute profile, query count, output morphology (gibberish-adjacent vs fluent), and failure modes.\n\n2. Attacker strength: operationalize as multi-dimensional but with a single primary \"budget\" ladder, and hold \"access\" as a separate crossed factor where feasible:\n   - Budget: number of target-model query iterations (PAIR: max iterations × attacker trees; AutoDAN: GA generations × population). E.g., low/mid/high = 0.25x/1x/4x of canonical budget. Canonical: AutoDAN ~ gen 120 × pop 40 (~ published), PAIR 20 iterations × 3 attacker streams.\n   - Access: white-box (loss/gradient available — AutoDAN can use it, plus token-level access enables things) vs black-box (query-only text out). PAIR is intrinsically black-box → access is confounded with style. Flag.\n   - Attacker model capability (for PAIR: attacker LLM strength, e.g. small vs large attacker). Applies only to PAIR → nested in style, not crossed.\n   - Seed/variance: multiple random seeds per (query, cell) → within-cell replication.\n   So attacker strength is partly nested-in-style rather than crossed. Important identifiability note.\n\n3. Defense strength: tiers, ordered but not interval:\n   D0 none (raw instruct, minimal system prompt)\n   D1 system-prompt hardening (static, no compute)\n   D2 input-side filtering (perplexity/style-classifier filter — e.g. PPL threshold, SelfDefend-style; note: this class of defense is *definitionally* hostile to token-level attacks and nearly inert vs fluent PAIR → guaranteed interaction, which is exactly what the question asks, but also means the \"defense strength ordering\" isn't monotone across styles → defense strength is not a single scalar; recommend treating defense as *type × dose*, not a single ordinal ladder)\n   D3 output-side moderation/classifier (Llama-Guard-style judge refusal)\n   D4 alignment training level: RLHF-baseline vs strengthened alignment (e.g. safety fine-tuned/R2D/harmlessness-tuned variant) — different mechanism (weight-level) from D1-D3 (inference-time wrappers).\n   Recommend factorizing: alignment_stage (2: baseline, strengthened) crossed with inference_defense (4: none/system/filter/moderation). That's 8 cells but each is mechanistically coherent, and lets \"defense strength\" be derived post hoc rather than assumed.\n\n4. Previously tested tricks (novelty/exposure): this is the trickiest. Operationalize as a factor with levels:\n   - \"novel\": attack components generated fresh in-study, not in the model's training corpus and not in public libraries (time-split / canary-tagged, unlaunched)\n   - \"previously publicized\": attacks drawn from a frozen public library (published jailbreak templates, published AutoDAN outputs) with documented publication date > model training cutoff where possible\n   - \"previously tested against this target\" (adaptive/known): attacks that failed against this exact target in a preceding phase and were then reused/recombined — models a defender who has already seen them.\n   Critical confound: novelty is confounded with (a) corpus contamination/memorization, (b) attack quality (freshly optimized vs curated), and (c) the defense's exposure (a defense fine-tuned on public attacks). So exposure must be crossed with defense, and \"previously tested\" must be operationally defined by a concrete, verifiable provenance ledger.\n\nDatasets: two candidate meanings. Recommend: harm-benchmark query sets as the primary blocking factor (AdvBench, TDC23/HarmBench core, plus a benign utility control set), with topic category (violence/illegal/fraud/privacy/hate/...) as a stratifying covariate within them. Also a benign control set to measure defense cost (refusal on benign = utility loss), so ASR alone isn't misleading.\n\nDesign type: not fully crossed. Fully crossed: 2 styles × 3 budgets × 3 accesses? × 8 defense × 3 novelty × 2 datasets × N queries — explodes and includes infeasible cells (PAIR has no white-box variant). Recommend:\n- Split-plot / blocked structure: attack style is the \"whole-plot\" factor (a run's compute profile is set by style), with attacker-strength ladder nested within style; defense × novelty × dataset crossed at the query level (cheap, since defense variations can be evaluated on cached attack outputs where the attack prompt is fixed).\n- Key efficiency trick: many cells can be evaluated on *frozen attack artifacts*. AutoDAN's optimization is against a defense; if we fix the attack artifact (the optimized string) and then test it under 8 defenses, we decouple attack generation cost from defense evaluation cost. That's crucial: D2 filter can also *block* the prompt before it reaches the model, so evaluating a frozen artifact under each defense is legitimate. But note: it breaks adaptivity — the attacker is not responding to the defense. That is the key limitation: frozen-artifact evaluation answers \"does a fixed attack transfer across defenses\", not \"does a stronger attacker beat a stronger defense when the attacker adapts\". Resolve with a two-tier protocol: Tier A (frozen artifact matrix, cheap, full factorial) and Tier B (adaptive, attacker optimizes against the actual defense — the honest estimate; expensive; restricted to a fraction, e.g. 1 defense pair × 2 budgets × subset of queries per style). Report the Tier A − Tier B gap as an estimator of adaptivity bias. That's a nice, defensible design decision.\n- Also within-subject at the query level → query-level repeated measures → mixed model with query random intercept + by-query random slope for defense.\n\nUnit of analysis / ASR denominator: the query × attack-attempt (query-campaign) is the unit; ASR denominator = number of *valid, delivered* attack attempts (attempts blocked at input filter count as failures, not exclusions — this is the single most consequential scoring decision, because excluding filtered prompts would inflate ASR under D2). Numerator = judged successful harmful completions. Report two denominators: per-attempt ASR (query-level) and per-behavior ASR@k (any-of-k success, the attacker's realistic objective), plus the \"effective ASR under budget\" curve (ASR vs budget). Also report utility/collateral refusal rate on benign controls so defense-cost is visible.\n\nScoring: hybrid — (i) rule-based refusal/keyword detector as a cheap first pass (fast, symmetric across styles but weak on partial compliance), (ii) an LLM judge on a *rubric* (HarmBench-style semantic assessment) plus (iii) a small human-validated gold set (~300–500 items, stratified over style × defense) to calibrate. Judge bias is style-dependent: token-level attacks produce disfluent/partial outputs; prompt-level attacks produce fluent, hedged harmful content. A single judge will systematically mis-score one style. Mitigation: judge ensemble (2 model families), blind the judge to the condition (strip the adversarial suffix/preamble and any formatting that reveals condition, so the judge sees only the response + original harmful intent), calibrate against gold set and report differential misclassification rate as a sensitivity analysis (e.g. refit with judge-assigned labels perturbed by measured per-condition error rates). Also must exclude \"attack succeeded in getting text but not harmful content\" and handle AutoDAN-specific false-positive risk (the optimizer overfits to the judge → report both judge and human-verified ASR on the AutoDAN-heavy subset).\n\nAnalysis:\n- Primary: binomial logistic mixed-effects per attempt: success ~ style*budget*defense_type*alignment*novelty*dataset + (1+|query) + (1+|model) (+ random slope for defense by query). Report ORs with CIs, and predicted-probability (marginal means) contrasts rather than only p-values.\n- Because per-behavior ASR@k is bounded and saturates (ceiling effects at D0), also model on the log-odds of the ASR@k with beta-regression/TOBIT or use a two-stage: fit per (cell, behavior) Beta-binomial over seeds → hierarchical model. Interaction on the log-odds scale ≠ interaction on risk-difference scale (HHI vs additive-scale interaction) — must pre-register which scale \"interaction dynamics\" means, since a defense that pushes ASR to floor mechanically produces \"negative interaction\" (sub-multiplicative) with no mechanism. This is a real identifiability/interpretation flag.\n- Budget dimension: model ASR vs budget as a dose-response (monotone curve; e.g. log-budget term), giving \"queries-to-50%-ASR\" (Q50) and area-under-attack-curve as interpretable effect sizes. This turns \"attacker power × defense robustness\" into a comparison of *budgets required*, which is the meaningful dynamic: defense shifts the attack curve rightward; interaction = differential rightward shift by style.\n- Multiple targets: model as a fixed blocking factor with ≥2-3 model families × sizes; model×defense×style interaction tells you whether robustness is model-specific.\n- Controls: (a) refusal-baseline / \"attack-free\" condition (direct harmful query, no transformation) per defense — anchors the defense's intrinsic refusal; (b) benign control prompts under each defense to estimate over-refusal; (c) random-perturbation baseline (shuffled AutoDAN suffix, PAIR template without optimization loop = \"one-shot\") to separate the effect of *optimization* from *surface form*; (d) judge-negative controls (gibberish outputs) to check judge false positives; (e) held-out attack seeds.\n- Uncertainty: cluster-bootstrap by query and by attack seed; report design effect/ICC; report per-cell n and effective sample size; simulate power for the highest-order interaction (likely underpowered — pre-register that 4-way interaction is exploratory).\n- Replication: 3 seeds; multiple judge versions.\n\nIdentifiability flags:\n1. Access (white-box vs black-box) is confounded with style given AutoDAN/PAIR only — cannot estimate access × defense separately from style × defense without adding a third method (e.g. GCG white-box token-level, and TAP/AutoDAN-prompt-level black-box). Recommend a 2×2 style × access with 4 methods if feasible, else declare access non-identifiable.\n2. \"Defense strength\" as a single ordinal ladder is not identifiable — mechanisms are non-commutative and non-monotone across styles. Use type × (dose within type).\n3. Novelty/exposure is confounded with attack-quality and contamination unless the novelty manipulation holds the optimization process fixed (same pipeline, same budget; only the *library provenance* varies). Also \"previously tested\" against the same target is confounded with the target's refusal history. Requires provenance ledger and time-split.\n4. Attacker-strength ladder is style-specific (GA generations ≠ PAIR iterations); cross-style comparison of \"attacker strength\" needs a common currency = number of target-model queries (query-normalized budget), plus report per-token compute. Otherwise \"attacker strength\" is measured on different scales by style → the style × strength interaction is confounded with the scale mismatch.\n5. Adaptive vs frozen-artifact evaluation: in the frozen matrix, defense effect is confounded with the fact the attacker never saw the defense. Non-identifiable within Tier A alone; Tier B resolves but is expensive.\n6. ASR denominator after input-filter blocking: not identifiable unless the blocking outcome is logged as an explicit third outcome class (delivered / blocked / refused / harmful). Recommend multinomial outcome (blocked / refused / non-harmful / harmful), analyzed as competing-risks-style decomposition — the cleanest way to attribute ASR drops to filtering vs refusal.\n7. Ceiling/floor at D0 and D3 saturations make multiplicative interaction uninterpretable; must report additive-scale interaction (RERI / marginal-effects differences) alongside OR.\n8. \"Across datasets\" — dataset differences bundle topic mix, harm severity, phrasing style, and language; dataset × style interaction is confounded with topic × style unless topic is modeled as a covariate nested within dataset.\n\nCompute realism: no GPU here, no network, no code — all of the above is design-only; the estimate of query volume should be given (e.g., N_behaviors=100, cells, attempts, ~queries per attempt) as a planning calc in the final design.\n\nAssumptions to list: meaning of datasets; which models; API vs local judge; defense implementations available; novelty representation; budget per cell; compute.\n\nAlso note the topics references (neurips2024/97431/11:setup_17, setup_18) — these documents are NOT in the repo; the brief gave only the track.md note. So source-supported setup details (the actual paper's setup) are unavailable to me; I must flag that any setup specifics (model names, defense names, dataset names, ASR judge) are assumptions unless the researcher supplies those topic docs.\n\nQuestion budget is 6 — I should list my top clarification questions (up to 6) in the plan.\n\nLet me write the handoff. It should be reasonably thorough but structured. Required handoff stage = plan.\n\nCompute estimate: let's do a rough planning number to include. Say 150 behaviors × 2 styles × 3 budget levels × 8 defense combos (2 alignment × 4 inference) × 3 novelty × 3 datasets? That's huge. Instead: Tier A frozen artifacts: 150 behaviors × 2 styles × 3 novelty × 3 seeds = 2,700 optimized artifacts (each expensive: AutoDAN 120×40=4,800 forward-equivalent evals; PAIR 20×3 = 60 target queries). Then each artifact × 8 defenses × 3 datasets-don't-matter (dataset is defined by the behavior) = 21,600 target inferences + judge calls. Manageable. Tier B adaptive: 30 behaviors × 2 styles × 2 budgets × subset of defenses (4) × 2 seeds ≈ 960 full attack runs — the dominant cost. Good. I'll put approximate numbers in the plan outline but keep the full cost model for the result stage.\n\nNow write.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## 1) Environment inspection — what actually exists\n\nVerified by direct probe; the environment is empty apart from the task brief.\n\n- `/workspace` contains exactly one file: `task.json` (637 bytes). No repo, no code, no `.git` anywhere on the filesystem (`find / -name .git` → empty).\n- `task.json` carries only the question plus `documents.track.md = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"` Nothing else.\n- The two `researcher_topics` (`neurips2024/97431/11:setup_17`, `.../setup_18`) are **not present on disk**. I searched for `track.md`, `setup_1*`, `*neurips*` across the filesystem: zero hits. So the source paper's concrete setup (models, defense names, datasets, ASR judge, budgets) is unavailable to me; everything of that nature below is an assumption, not a reading of the source.\n- No model weights: `ls /data /models /opt` → nothing; home dir empty; no `~/.cache/huggingface`.\n- No attack harnesses: no AutoDAN/PAIR/GCG/TAP code, no judge/scoring code, no datasets.\n- No ML stack: `torch`, `transformers`, `numpy`, `scipy`, `statsmodels`, `pandas`, `openai`, `anthropic` all MISSING (system Python 3.14.4; only OS/utility packages installed).\n- No GPU (`nvidia-smi` fails: no driver), no network (DNS resolution fails; consistent with the `network: disabled` spec). CPU: 224 cores, ~2 TB RAM.\n\nPlainly: **nothing is present.** This is design-only, as the track note says. I will not assume a stack exists; the design must specify what would need to be provisioned (target models, attacker LLM, AutoDAN + PAIR reference implementations, filter/moderation defenses, judge access, harm benchmarks) rather than claim any of it is available.\n\n## 2) Outline-level approach (not the deliverable)\n\n### 2.1 Factor set and operationalization\n\nFour factors, but they are not symmetric — two are crossed, one is nested, one is arguably not a single factor at all.\n\n**Style (crossed, 2 levels, whole-plot):** token-level AutoDAN (genetic/prefix optimization over a natural-language-readable adversarial affix, fluency+perplexity-constrained) vs. prompt-level PAIR (attacker LLM iteratively proposes/refines semantic jailbreak prompts). These differ in output morphology (disfluent/partial vs. fluent/hedged), query count, and failure modes, so they are treated as structural whole-plots, not interchangeable levels of a nuisance factor.\n\n**Attacker strength (nested within style, not crossed):** three sub-dimensions, deliberately kept separate.\n- *Budget*: number of **target-model queries**, which is the only common currency across styles. GA generations × population for AutoDAN; iterations × parallel attacker streams for PAIR. Mapped to low/mid/high ≈ 0.25×/1×/4× of canonical published settings, with canonical ≈ 4.8k evaluation queries (AutoDAN, 120 gen × 40 pop) vs. ≈ 60 target queries (PAIR, 20 iters × 3 streams).\n- *Access*: white-box (loss/gradient) vs. black-box (text-out only).\n- *Attacker capability*: attacker-LLM size/quality — **applies only to PAIR**.\nBecause GA generations and PAIR iterations are not the same unit, \"attacker strength\" must be reported query-normalized (queries, plus token-compute) or the style × strength interaction is confounded with a measurement-scale mismatch. Seeds give within-cell replication.\n\n**Defense strength — I recommend rejecting the ordinal ladder.** \"None → system prompt → PPL/filter → RLHF vs. strengthened alignment\" is not one ordered scale: input filtering is *definitionally* lethal to token-level affixes and nearly inert against fluent PAIR prompts, so the ordering is non-monotone across styles and \"defense strength\" cannot be a scalar. Factorize mechanistically instead:\n- `alignment_stage` (2): shipped RLHF baseline vs. strengthened alignment (weight-level change);\n- `inference_defense` (4, nested dose within type): none / static system-prompt hardening / input-side filter (perplexity + style/classifier block) / output-side moderation classifier.\n2 × 4 = 8 coherent cells. \"Defense strength\" becomes a *derived* post-hoc summary (mean ASR suppression within type), not a primitive.\n\n**Previously-tested-trick exposure (crossed, 3 levels, provenance-defined):** *novel* (artifacts generated in-study by the same pipeline, unpublished, corpus-post-cutoff, canary-tagged) vs. *publicized* (frozen snapshot of a public attack library with documented dates) vs. *previously-tested* (artifacts that failed against this exact target in a preceding phase, then reused/recombined — models a defender who has already seen them). The manipulation must hold pipeline and budget **fixed** and vary only library provenance, else novelty is confounded with optimization quality. Requires a provenance ledger.\n\n**Datasets (blocking/stratifying, not a treatment):** primary = harm-benchmark query sets (e.g. AdvBench, HarmBench/TDC23 core) with **topic category** (violence, fraud, privacy, hate, ...) as a stratifying covariate *nested within dataset*, plus a **benign control set** to measure defense cost (over-refusal). Without the benign set, ASR suppression under strong defenses is uninterpretable.\n\n### 2.2 Design type: blocked split-plot with a two-tier compute split — explicitly *not* fully crossed\n\nFully crossed is both infeasible and partly undefined (PAIR has no white-box variant; attacker capability is undefined for AutoDAN). I favor:\n\n- **Whole-plot** = attack style (sets the per-run compute profile). **Attacker-strength ladder nested within style.** **Defense × exposure crossed at the artifact level**, cheap because of the trick below.\n- **Tier A — frozen-artifact matrix (cheap, near-full factorial):** optimize the attack artifact once *independent of the defense*, then evaluate the frozen artifact under all 8 defense cells. Decouples attack-generation cost from defense-evaluation cost; input-blocking is a legitimate evaluation outcome, not a violation. ≈ 150 behaviors × 2 styles × 3 exposures × 3 seeds ≈ 2.7k artifacts → × 8 defenses ≈ 22k target inferences + judge calls.\n- **Tier B — adaptive attack (expensive, deliberately fractional):** attacker optimizes *against the actual deployed defense* — the honest estimate. Restricted to a subset (e.g. 30 behaviors × 2 styles × 2 budgets × 4 defenses × 2 seeds ≈ 960 full attack runs), which dominates cost.\n- **The Tier A − Tier B gap is itself a reported quantity:** an estimate of adaptivity bias in frozen-transfer studies. This is the design's main payoff for the \"interaction dynamics\" question.\n- Within-subject at the query level (every behavior seen under every defense/exposure) buys large power for defense effects at no extra data-collection cost beyond inference.\n\n### 2.3 Unit of analysis, ASR denominator, unbiased scoring\n\n- **Unit:** one attack *attempt* on one behavior (behavior × cell × seed). Behaviors are the random/clustering unit; ASR uncertainty is bootstrapped by behavior and by seed, with the reported design effect/ICC.\n- **Outcome is multinomial, not binary:** `blocked-at-input-filter` / `refused` / `answered-but-not-harmful` / `harmful-completion`. This is how ASR drops are attributed to *filtering* vs. *refusal* rather than being silently pooled — otherwise the D2 filter effect is unidentifiable from the model's own refusal.\n- **Denominator = all attempts initiated, including filter-blocked ones.** Excluding blocked prompts would mechanically inflate ASR precisely under input filtering, i.e. the scoring rule would reverse the effect we are testing. This is the single most consequential scoring decision.\n- Report three complementary numerators: per-attempt ASR; **per-behavior ASR@k** (any-of-k success — the attacker's realistic objective); and **ASR-vs-budget curve** summaries (`queries-to-50%-ASR`, area under attack curve). Plus benign-set over-refusal as a paired cost metric.\n- **Scoring:** rule-based refusal detector (cheap, style-symmetric, weak on partial compliance) → LLM judge on a HarmBench-style semantic rubric → human-validated gold set (~300–500 items **stratified over style × defense**, deliberately over-sampling AutoDAN disfluent outputs). Judge bias is style-dependent by construction: fluent-hedged PAIR output and disfluent partial AutoDAN output are mis-scored in *opposite* directions by a single judge.\n- **Bias control:** judge ensemble from two model families; blind the judge by stripping the adversarial affix/preamble and condition-revealing formatting so it sees only (original harmful intent, response); report per-condition misclassification rates vs. gold and refit ASR with those measured error rates as a sensitivity analysis. Explicitly guard against AutoDAN's known failure mode of optimizer-overfitting-to-the-judge by reporting judge-scored and human-verified ASR on the AutoDAN-heavy subset.\n\n### 2.4 Analysis plan and what \"interaction\" is actually identifiable\n\n- **Primary:** binomial GLMM on delivered attempts, `success ~ style * budget * inference_defense * alignment * exposure * dataset + (1+inference_defense|behavior) + (1|model) + (1|seed)`; predicted marginal means with CIs rather than p-value hunting; Beta-binomial hierarchical stage for ASR@k, which is bounded and saturates.\n- **Scale pre-registration (essential):** report interaction on **both** the log-odds/multiplicative scale (OR ratios) **and** the additive/marginal-effects scale (risk-difference contrasts, RERI). At D0→ASR≈1 and moderation→ASR≈0, ceilings/floors mechanically produce \"sub-multiplicative interaction\" with no mechanism. \"Interaction dynamics\" must be defined on a declared scale or the headline finding is an artifact.\n- **Dynamics reframed as budgets:** the interpretable statement of attacker-power × defense-robustness interaction is a *differential rightward shift of the ASR-vs-query-budget curve* per style per defense — i.e. how many queries each defense buys, and whether that protection degrades with attacker budget.\n- **Across datasets:** dataset × style interaction interpreted only with topic modeled as nested covariate, plus explicit heterogeneity reporting (per-dataset re-fit, I²/Q), since dataset bundles topic mix, severity and phrasing.\n- **Controls:** (a) no-attack direct-query baseline per defense (intrinsic refusal anchor); (b) benign control set (over-refusal); (c) **surface-form controls** — shuffled AutoDAN affix and one-shot PAIR template without the optimization loop, separating the effect of *optimization* from *surface form*; (d) judge negative controls (gibberish) for false positives; (e) held-out attack seeds for out-of-sample artifact generalization.\n- **Uncertainty:** cluster bootstrap over behaviors and seeds; per-cell n and effective sample size; simulated power for the highest-order interactions with the 4-way declared exploratory a priori.\n\n## 3) Consequential assumptions (all unverified — nothing here is source-supported)\n\n1. \"Datasets\" = harm-benchmark query sets, topic-stratified, plus a benign control set. The brief does not say; it could mean domain splits.\n2. Open-weight target models (≥2 families × sizes) evaluated locally; closed-API targets excluded (no network here, and API ToS/terms-of-service constraints on jailbreak artifacts).\n3. Defense implementations for all 8 cells are buildable (system prompts, a PPL/classifier filter, a moderation classifier, a strengthened-alignment checkpoint). Whether such a checkpoint exists is unknown.\n4. An LLM judge of sufficient quality is reachable; in this environment nothing is.\n5. \"Previously tested tricks\" = a **reused/curated attack library with a provenance ledger** (novel vs. publicized vs. previously-failed-against-this-target), *not* merely held-out random seeds. This is my proposal, not the brief.\n6. A realistic per-cell query budget can be fixed (Tier A ≈ 2×10^4 inferences; Tier B dominates at ≈ 10^5–10^6 target queries) — needs your compute ceiling.\n7. Harmful-content policy permits storing/evaluating jailbreak artifacts in the study setting.\n8. Canonical published AutoDAN/PAIR hyperparameters are the budget reference point.\n\n## 4) Flags: factors I do NOT think are identifiable as proposed — resolve before drafting\n\n- **F1 Access × defense is confounded with style.** With only AutoDAN and PAIR, white-vs-black-box cannot be separated from token-vs-prompt-level. Either add methods to form a 2×2 (e.g. GCG for white-box token-level, TAP for black-box prompt-level) or declare access non-identifiable.\n- **F2 \"Defense strength\" as one ordinal ladder is not identifiable** across styles (non-monotone: filters kill affix attacks, spare fluent ones). I propose replacing it with type × dose as above — needs your sign-off since it changes the question's stated factor.\n- **F3 Frozen-artifact defense effect is confounded with non-adaptivity.** Tier A alone cannot separate \"defense is robust\" from \"attacker never saw the defense.\" Only Tier B resolves it, and it is affordable only fractionally.\n- **F4 Filter-blocking vs. model refusal** is unidentifiable with a binary ASR. Resolved only by the 4-class outcome in 2.3.\n- **F5 Novelty vs. contamination vs. attack quality** are entangled unless pipeline+budget are held fixed and only provenance varies; also the *defender's* exposure to publicized attacks is uncontrolled for pretrained defenses.\n- **F6 Attacker-strength units differ by style**; cross-style strength comparisons are confounded with the unit mismatch unless everything is query-normalized.\n- **F7 Dataset × style is confounded with topic × style** unless topic is modeled within dataset.\n- **F8 Multiplicative interaction is uninterpretable near 0/1**; the interaction claim is scale-dependent by construction.\n\n## 5) Six questions for you (budget = 6; ordered by design impact)\n\n1. **F2:** OK to replace the single ordinal defense-strength ladder with `alignment_stage × inference_defense(type)`?\n2. **F1:** Is expanding beyond AutoDAN/PAIR (to break the access/style confound) in scope, or is the study strictly two methods?\n3. **Compute ceiling:** approximate total target-query budget available — this decides the Tier A/Tier B split and how fractional the design is.\n4. **\"Datasets\":** harm benchmarks (which ones) with topic stratification, or domain splits of one pool? And is a benign utility-control set wanted?\n5. **\"Previously tested tricks\":** does the source support a novel / publicized / previously-failed three-level provenance manipulation, or is it binary (novel vs. reused library)?\n6. **Target models and judge:** which models and which ASR judge does `setup_17`/`setup_18` specify? I cannot read those documents from this environment, and this determines whether local judging, a judge ensemble, and a human gold set are even possible.\n", "evidence": "Inspection commands run in /workspace (all read-only):\n1. `ls -la /workspace; find /workspace -maxdepth 3` → only `task.json` (637 B). Repository otherwise empty.\n2. `cat /workspace/task.json` → question text + `\"track.md\": \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"` No setup documents.\n3. `python3 -c \"import torch, transformers\"` → ModuleNotFoundError. `pip list` → only system/OS packages (ansible, cryptography, Jinja2, ...); no ML/data stack.\n4. Explicit import probe: numpy, scipy, statsmodels, pandas, openai, anthropic → all MISSING.\n5. `nvidia-smi` → \"failed because it couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\" (no GPU). `python3 --version` → 3.14.4.\n6. `ls /data /models /opt`, `ls ~`, `ls ~/.cache/huggingface` → no data, no models, no HF cache (home dir empty).\n7. `find / -xdev -maxdepth 4 (-iname track.md -o -iname \"setup_1*\" -o -iname \"*neurips*\")` → empty; the two researcher_topics documents are not on disk. `find / -name .git -type d` → empty (no repos). `find / ... -iname *autodan* -o -iname *pair* -o -iname *jailbreak*` → empty (no attack harnesses).\n8. Network probe via urllib to huggingface.co → URLError \"Temporary failure in name resolution\", consistent with `network: disabled`.\n9. Resources: `nproc` → 224; `free -g` → ~2011 GB RAM.\n\nThese are environment-scoping checks only; no experiment was performed and no outcome is claimed.", "assumptions": ["Task is design-only: nothing is installed or runnable here, so the design must specify a stack to be provisioned rather than assume one (verified by inspection).", "The source documents behind researcher_topics setup_17/setup_18 specify models/datasets/judge but are not readable from this environment; every setup-specific detail in the outline is my assumption, not a source reading.", "'Datasets' is assumed to mean harm-benchmark query sets with topic-level stratification plus a benign control set, rather than domain splits of a single pool.", "Target models are assumed open-weight and locally served; API targets are excluded.", "'Previously tested tricks' is assumed operationalizable as a 3-level provenance manipulation (novel / publicized / previous