{"id": "d1aea7f7-ab15-4f85-8e2d-59d5146a2726", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T00:17:42.790168", "source": "user", "parent_id": "cd05707d-2c53-4367-b7f9-df93b2062e82", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Good inspection — it matches the project context (design-only, no network, no GPU, no ML stack), and your candor about setup_17/setup_18 being unreadable is correct: I cannot read them either. The only setup lines I can confirm as fixed are token-level = AutoDAN and prompt-level = PAIR. Everything else of that nature is researcher direction or assumption, and must be labeled as such. Proceed to the full design under the resolutions below.\\n\\n**Answers to your six questions (binding scope decisions):**\\n\\n1. **F2 — approved, with a constraint.** Replace the ordinal ladder with `alignment_stage (2) × inference_defense (type × dose)`. The non-monotonicity across styles is precisely the phenomenon I want measured, so a scalar \\\"defense strength\\\" would destroy the interesting result. Constraint: since the question is phrased in terms of defense strength, also publish a *derived* post-hoc summary (within-type ASR suppression), clearly labeled as derived, for comparability with prior work.\\n\\n2. **F1 — do not expand the core design.** AutoDAN and PAIR are fixed requirements, so the access×style confound remains. Declare access non-identifiable in the primary analysis rather than pretending to estimate it. You may describe one clearly labelled optional identification probe (white-box token-level and/or black-box prompt-level comparator) in the feasibility/extension section as a sensitivity arm; it must not enter the primary model and must be flagged as beyond the fixed requirement set.\\n\\n3. **Compute ceiling — I have no number to give you; that is a genuine unknown, not an oversight.** No compute exists here and none is stated in the brief. So do not pick a single figure: specify the design parametrically with a **cost model plus decision rule** — per-cell target-query and judge-call counts, then 2–3 named regimes (full / standard / stripped fallback) where the fallback still identifies the primary style × defense × budget interactions. State Tier B sizing as a function of the ceiling.\\n\\n4. **Datasets — your reading is right, and yes to the benign control.** Harm-benchmark query sets with topic nested within dataset as a stratifying covariate, plus a benign utility set scored on the same items as a paired over-refusal cost metric.\\n\\n5. **Previously tested tricks — three levels acceptable, but pre-register the primary contrast as binary.** Novel vs previously-tested-in-study is the estimable, well-controlled contrast; publicized is reported as a third level with the contamination caveat (pretrained defenders may already have seen it) stated as a limitation. Pipeline and query budget fixed, provenance varies, provenance ledger maintained. I accept this operationalization is your proposal, not the brief's.\\n\\n6. **Models and judge — unspecified; keep as assumptions.** Do not attribute model/dataset/judge choices to the source. Design for ≥2 open-weight families × ≥2 scales, a two-family judge ensemble, blinding via artifact/preamble stripping before judging, and a style × defense-stratified human gold set whose measured per-condition error rates feed a sensitivity refit. Add an explicit degraded-mode single-judge variant — it is the realistic constraint and it interacts with AutoDAN judge-overfitting.\\n\\n**Also required in the deliverable:**\\n- A fixed-requirements-vs-assumptions table, so my directives above are never mistaken for source content.\\n- The 4-class outcome and the all-attempts-initiated denominator kept prominent, with your argument for why the alternative mechanically reverses the filter effect. This is the load-bearing scoring decision.\\n- Interaction reported on both multiplicative and additive scales, with your budget-curve reframing (\\\"queries bought per defense, and whether protection degrades with attacker budget\\\") as the **primary** statement of interaction dynamics rather than a supplement. That reframing is the strongest element of the outline.\\n- The Tier A − Tier B gap reported as a first-class result (adaptivity bias in frozen-transfer studies), not a footnote.\\n- A feasibility section stating plainly that nothing is provisioned, enumerating what must be built, and treating the strengthened-alignment checkpoint as the riskiest dependency: if no such checkpoint exists, state exactly what the design degrades to. Include the cost model from (3).\\n\\nStay design-only; do not run anything or claim results.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "550e9e19-ab72-45d1-b5d8-e407eb81a4db", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T00:28:12.169297", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "d1aea7f7-ab15-4f85-8e2d-59d5146a2726", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Understood — proceeding to the full design. First, let me ground the cost model and the power/arithmetic claims in actual planning computations (design-time arithmetic only, not an experiment)."}], "reasoning_content": "I need to produce the full experiment design as stage=result. Let me build a cost model with concrete parametric numbers, decision rules, regimes.\n\nLet me carefully construct the cost model.\n\nCanonical budget references:\n- AutoDAN: published settings roughly generations G=120, population/offspring... Actually AutoDAN (Liu et al., NeurIPS 2024) uses genetic algorithm with population size 40, generations 120, elite 10, offspring 20, mutation rate. Each generation requires evaluating fitness of each individual on target model → 1 generation = ~40 target queries (plus 20 offspring). Let's use: per-behavior AutoDAN = G × P = 120 × 40 = 4,800 target queries at 1× budget; plus 30 initial seeds.\n- PAIR: N attacker templates = 20 (attacker streams), M max iterations = 20, B=1 attacker proposal per stream per iteration → target queries = N × M = 400 at 1×. Actually standard PAIR: N=20 attacker prompts, M=20 iterations, queries ≈ N×M = 400 target queries; attacker LLM calls = same number (each target query preceded by one attacker call). I earlier said 60 — let me correct: PAIR canonical in the paper (Chao et al. 2023) uses N=20, M=3 or M=20 with early stopping at success. To be safe, I'll define PAIR 1× = N=20 streams × M=20 iterations = up to 400 target queries with early stopping on success (realized mean lower). I'll state it as an explicit parameter and note it's an assumption. Since I said 60 earlier, I should correct it and note the correction — good faith.\n\nActually honesty: I should flag that I'm now correcting the earlier figure (20×3=60 → N=20,M=20=400) as an assumption either way, since the source is unreadable. Good.\n\nCost model:\nDefine per attempt (one behavior × one attack cell × one seed):\n- Q_A(1×) = 4,800 target queries for AutoDAN; Q_P(1×) = 400 target queries for PAIR (realized ~lower with early stop). Budget multipliers b ∈ {0.25, 1, 4}.\n- But: with defense present, early stopping and filter-blocking change realized queries. Introduce realized-query factor.\n\nTier A (frozen artifact transfer): optimize once per (behavior, style, exposure, seed) at the reference condition (defense-none under RLHF baseline), then replay the frozen artifact across all 8 defense cells → 1 target query per replay (no optimization). So Tier A cost = generation cost + replay cost.\n\nGeneration cost Tier A = B_beh × S_styles × E_exposures × R_seeds × Q_style(1×)\nTake B=150 behaviors? Let's use parametric N_b.\n\nLet me write:\n- N_b behaviors per dataset, D datasets (assume 2 harm datasets), plus N_ben benign controls.\n- Tier A cells: style(2) × exposure(3) × seed(3) × alignment(2)... hmm — careful. Exposure/novelty artifacts: novel artifacts must be generated. Publicized library artifacts already exist (zero generation cost). Previously-tested artifacts come from a preceding phase's failures. So generation cost applies mainly to novel level.\n- alignment_stage doubles the target model inventory (2 model variants per model config) — model-variant count M_v = families(F≥2) × scales(S≥2) × alignment(2). That's a big cost multiplier. Cost = per-model inference.\n\nLet me define the cost model cleanly:\n\nTarget-model inventory: N_m = F × S × A where F=2 families, S=2 scales, A=2 alignment stages → 8 model variants (this is huge for local serving; note it). Could restrict alignment-strengthened to a subset. Note as a decision lever.\n\nTier A replay queries:\nQ_A_replay = N_b(total harm behaviors across datasets) × N_style(2) × N_exp(3) × N_seed(3) × N_def(4 inference defenses) × N_model_variant(N_m) \nNote: defense \"none\" is one of the 4 inference_defense levels; and alignment is a model-level factor so it's in N_m. So replay cells = N_b × 2 × 3 × 3 × 4 × N_m.\nWith N_b=300 (2 datasets × 150), that's 300×2×3×3×4×8 = 172,800 single-generation queries. Each generation ~ up to 512 tokens. Plus benign set replay (no attack, style N/A → 1 benign pass: N_ben × 4 × N_m).\n\nTier A optimization (generation) cost — done once per (behavior, style, exposure=novel, seed) against the reference defense cell (none, baseline alignment, primary model family):\n- AutoDAN: N_b × 1 × 3 seeds × 4800 = 300×3×4800 = 4.32M queries. Dominant.\n- PAIR: 300 × 3 × 400 = 360k target queries + 360k attacker calls.\nAlso artifacts must be optimized per alignment stage? No — Tier A defines artifacts as frozen and transferred; optimization is at reference condition. But that means for strengthened alignment, all Tier A is transfer-only. That's fine and is a stated limitation.\n\nAdditionally budget ladder: Tier A optimization at 3 budget levels {0.25,1,4} to get ASR-vs-budget curve. Multiplies generation cost by (0.25+1+4)=5.25 relative to 1× → 4.32M × 5.25 = 22.7M for AutoDAN. That's the real driver. So: the budget ladder is what makes this expensive → hence nested-budget trick: for AutoDAN, a single GA run of length 4× can be *checkpointed* at 0.25× and 1× → budget ladder for AutoDAN costs only 4× (not 5.25×) and gives nested/lower-budget subsets nearly free. Great efficiency point. For PAIR, iterations are also sequential → checkpoint at iteration thresholds, nearly free (though not exactly identical to independent low-budget runs because of early stopping; note it, and reserve a small independent low-budget replicate to check the checkpointing equivalence). Excellent — that's a real design insight.\n\nSo AutoDAN generation at 4× budget with checkpoints at 0.25×/1×/4× = 300 × 3 seeds × 19,200 = 17.3M target queries. Still dominant.\n\nTier B adaptive: attacker optimizes against the