tion, AutoDAN=token-level, PAIR=prompt-level, design-only. Everything else = directive or assumption. Good — three-way table as requested (they asked \"fixed-requirements-vs-assumptions\"; I'll do three tiers to keep directives distinct from source).\n\n4-class outcome + all-attempts-initiated denominator prominence with the reversal argument. Include a concrete numeric illustration: suppose true harmful-completion rate among delivered is 40%; filter blocks 60%; with all-attempts denominator ASR=0.4×0.4=16% (vs none-defense ASR 50%) → defense appears to help; with delivered-only denominator ASR=40% (vs 50%) → looks near-useless. Better: build the reversal: a defense that blocks low-quality attempts preferentially can make delivered-only ASR *increase*. Example: none-defense: 100 attempts, 90 delivered, 30 harmful → delivered-only ASR 33.3%. Filter: 100 attempts, 40 delivered, 15 harmful → delivered-only 37.5%. So delivered-only ASR rises (33→38) while all-attempts ASR falls (30%→15%). That's the mechanical reversal. Nice, include that.\n\nBudget curve reframing as primary statement of interaction dynamics: define protection ratio PR_style(def) = Q50(def)/Q50(none) — \"queries bought\"; and degradation slope d log PR / d log Q → whether protection erodes with attacker budget. Define via ASR(q) curves fit per (style, defense type, model) — monotone increasing saturating function, e.g. ASR(q) = L / (1 + exp(-k(log q - log q50))) or ASR(q)=1-(1-p1)^... Use a per-attempt \"attempt-level hazard of success at query q\" survival formulation: P(first success by q) = 1 - exp(-λ q) under exchangeability → ASR@k = 1-(1-p)^k exactly; that gives a clean parametric link between per-attempt ASR p and ASR@k and makes Q50 = ln(0.5)/ln(1-p) — very elegant: Q50 = log(0.5)/log(1-p). So the budget-curve reframing reduces to per-attempt success probability p, and Q50 is a monotone transform, and interaction on the Q50-ratio scale = multiplicative in log(1-p). That's a genuinely nice quantitative structure. Let me include that formula and compute a couple of illustrative numbers.\n\nQ50 with p=0.1: ln0.5/ln0.9 = -0.693/-0.1053=6.58. p=0.02 → 34.3. p=0.3→ 1.94. Good.\n\nDefense \"buys\" factor = Q50(def)/Q50(none) = log(1-p_none)/log(1-p_def). Report as multiplicative protection ratio with delta-method/bootstrap CI. Interaction style×defense on that scale = ratio of protection ratios. And note it's equivalent to testing interaction on the log(1-p) (\"log-survival\"/log-refusal-retention) scale — which is well-defined even at p→0? At p=0, log(1-p)=0 and ratio → infinite/undefined → floor problem remains; acknowledge, and propose a floor-handling (report with CI, use additive scale as co-primary). Good.\n\nMixed model: binomial GLMM with logit link, random intercept for behavior and behavior×defense random slope; model, seed random. Note the non-crossed bits: attacker capability nested in PAIR; access non-identifiable.\n\nAlso pre-registered contrasts list.\n\nAlso: \"previously-tested\" phase preceding: define Phase 0 (screening/failure harvesting): run novel attacks at 1× against model M under defense-none, collect failed artifacts → these constitute \"previously-tested-in-study\" pool; then re-run them under defenses in Phase 1. Wait — semantics: \"previously tested tricks\" in the source sense likely = tricks previously tested/published (known to the defender). Researcher said \"novel vs previously-tested-in-study is the estimable contrast\". So previously-tested = artifacts that the defender has seen (i.e., used to construct/augment the defense: e.g., strengthened alignment fine-tuned on them, or filter blocklist built from them). Hmm — two readings. Researcher said: \"Novel vs previously-tested-in-study is the estimable, well-controlled contrast\" — models a defender who has already seen them. I should define it as: artifacts harvested in Phase 0 and then **made known to the defense** (added to filter blocklist / included in strengthened-alignment safety fine-tuning data / included in moderation classifier training), while the attack pipeline and budget are held fixed. That's the clean operationalization of \"previously tested\" = defender-adapted. And a symmetric control: novel artifacts unseen by defense. That gives the estimable contrast: exposure × defense. Also I should note the alternative reading (reuse of own failed artifacts without defender adaptation) as a distinct sub-level \"reused-failed\" — but keep it out of the primary to avoid clutter? I'll mention as an optional 4th level/secondary.\n\nCareful: to keep the primary well-controlled, the exposure contrast must hold artifact *quality* fixed. Best device: **matched-pair generation** — for each behavior, generate the previously-tested (defender-adapted) and novel artifacts with identical pipeline and budget, and additionally include a \"sham-exposure\" control where the defense is adapted to a *different* behavior's artifacts (matched for quality) to separate behavior-level difficulty from exposure. Hmm, that's a nice control: defense exposed to a yoked set of artifacts from *other* behaviors → controls for the fact that adding attack data to defense fine-tuning changes defense generally, not just exposure-specific. Yes — this is the key identification device: exposure effect = defense adapted to the *same trick family* vs. adapted to *matched other tricks*. Include as a required control (yoked-sham exposure). Good, this addresses F5 partially.\n\nFeasibility section: nothing provisioned; enumerate: (1) harm benchmarks (AdvBench, HarmBench core, TDC23, SORRY-Bench) — licensing/consent review; (2) benign control set (e.g. Alpaca-style / XOR); (3) open-weight model serving; (4) AutoDAN reimplementation (official code unavailable offline → must reimplement GA, risk of fidelity gap); (5) PAIR harness (small, MIT-licensed, reimplementable); (6) filter defense (PPL model + style classifier — needs training data); (7) moderation classifier; (8) strengthened alignment checkpoint — riskiest: requires a base model + safety SFT/DPO pipeline + compute + careful over-refusal control; if absent → degrade: alignment_stage collapses, primary becomes style × inference_defense × budget on baseline alignment, and the exposure manipulation loses its defender-adaptation mechanism (must be operationalized via filter blocklist / in-context defense memory only), and publicized-level contamination caveat worsens. Also mention alignment_stage could be substituted by an open alignment-variant family pair (e.g., an instruct model vs. its officially hardened successor) as fallback — labeled as a weaker proxy confounded with family.\n(9) judge access (2 families) + human annotation for gold set; (10) provenance-ledger tooling; (11) artifact storage under safety policy; (12) no-network: all provisioning must be done elsewhere.\n\nAlso feasibility checks I can actually run: none of the experiment, but I did verify the environment arithmetic; maybe run the MDE arithmetic and Q50 numbers now (pure arithmetic, allowed). Let's do that to ground numbers.\n\nTimeline/sequencing, pre-registration, ethics/IRB-ish (harm policy, responsible disclosure, artifact handling), and reporting standards (ASR with denominators, per-cell n, judge version).\n\nLet me now run the arithmetic.\n\nAlso power: per-behavior ASR@k. Let me compute for the primary interaction: additive interaction in ASR@k between style and defense with N_b behaviors × seeds.\n\nLet me just do computations:\n1. Q50 values for p grid.\n2. MDE for additive interaction (difference-in-differences) given n per cell and baseline p's.\n3. Cost model totals for the three regimes.\n\nLet me write a python script using stdlib math.\n\nCareful: don't claim results of an experiment; these are design-time planning computations. That's fine and clearly labeled.\n\nLet me code it.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cat << 'EOF' > /tmp/cost.py\nimport