{"id": "18ce8481-37ce-4a67-bbc9-2fc0bddb1bd7", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T00:28:12.419580", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "8302abd9-20aa-43f2-8288-c3e2564b1405", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9a27cfcaf6805da3", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"cat << 'EOF' > /tmp/mech.py\\n# The mechanical-reversal argument: all-attempts vs delivered-only denominators.\\ndef show(label, attempts, delivered, harmful):\\n    print(f\\\"{label:28s} attempts={attempts:4d} delivered={delivered:4d} blocked={attempts-delivered:4d} \\\"\\n          f\\\"harmful={harmful:3d}  ASR_all={harmful/attempts:.3f}  ASR_delivered={harmful/delivered:.3f}\\\")\\nprint(\\\"--- Filter blocks the *weakest* attempts (quality-selective blocking) ---\\\")\\nshow(\\\"Defense: none\\\",      100, 90, 30)\\nshow(\\\"Defense: input-filter\\\",100, 40, 15)\\nprint(\\\"-> ASR_all: 0.300 -> 0.150 (defense helps, correct)\\\")\\nprint(\\\"-> ASR_delivered: 0.333 -> 0.375 (defense looks HARMFUL: mechanical reversal)\\\\n\\\")\\nprint(\\\"--- Filter blocks indiscriminately ---\\\")\\nshow(\\\"Defense: none\\\",      100, 90, 30)\\nshow(\\\"Defense: input-filter\\\",100, 45, 15)\\nEOF\\npython3 /tmp/mech.py\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.116, \"output\": \"--- Filter blocks the *weakest* attempts (quality-selective blocking) ---\\nDefense: none                attempts= 100 delivered=  90 blocked=  10 harmful= 30  ASR_all=0.300  ASR_delivered=0.333\\nDefense: input-filter        attempts= 100 delivered=  40 blocked=  60 harmful= 15  ASR_all=0.150  ASR_delivered=0.375\\n-> ASR_all: 0.300 -> 0.150 (defense helps, correct)\\n-> ASR_delivered: 0.333 -> 0.375 (defense looks HARMFUL: mechanical reversal)\\n\\n--- Filter blocks indiscriminately ---\\nDefense: none                attempts= 100 delivered=  90 blocked=  10 harmful= 30  ASR_all=0.300  ASR_delivered=0.333\\nDefense: input-filter        attempts= 100 delivered=  45 blocked=  55 harmful= 15  ASR_all=0.150  ASR_delivered=0.333\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "df910cdd-6268-466e-b7f0-8ffebfaeac8a", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "667d6430-9793-4075-a5ff-33276d280e2f", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T00:30:01.033344", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "18ce8481-37ce-4a67-bbc9-2fc0bddb1bd7", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Cost primitives and the reversal argument check out. Now the parametric cost model, power arithmetic, and regime sizing."}], "reasoning_content": "Now compute sample size / power for the primary interaction, and the parametric cost model with regimes.\n\nPower for a 2x2 interaction on the additive scale in ASR (two-proportion interaction). Let me compute required n per cell for detecting an interaction of given size, and conversely MDE given plausible cell counts.\n\nCell counts: Tier A frozen matrix: behaviors N_b × seeds. Each (style × exposure × defense-cell × dataset × budget) cell has N_b × seeds attempts. With N_b=150 behaviors and 3 seeds → 450 attempts/cell. Detectable interaction on additive scale: for a 2x2 interaction with proportions, SE of interaction ≈ sqrt(sum p(1-p)/n). Let me compute MDE.\n\nLet me just compute: interaction contrast = (p11 - p10) - (p01 - p00). SE = sqrt(Σ p(1-p)/n). With p ~ 0.2 average and n=450: SE = sqrt(4*0.2*0.8/450)= sqrt(0.64/450)=sqrt(0.001422)=0.0377. MDE at 80% power, alpha .05 two-sided: 2.8*SE = 0.106. So ~10.6 pp interaction detectable with 450 attempts/cell.\n\nBut clustering (behaviors) inflates SE by design effect sqrt(DEFF) where DEFF = 1+(m-1)*ICC with m = seeds per behavior (3) — actually clustering is by behavior; with multiple seeds per behavior, ICC across seeds. If ICC=0.1, m=3, DEFF=1.2, SE*1.1. Modest. But behaviors are crossed with all conditions, so behavior-level clustering is handled by random intercept, and the relevant SE is at the behavior level: effectively n_eff = N_b behaviors for between-condition contrasts? No — for within-behavior contrasts (defense), the relevant variance is behavior-level too since all behaviors get all defenses; the random intercept doesn't inflate within-behavior contrast SE much (paired design helps). Good.\n\nAlso compute for Tier B sizing: Tier B cost per behavior per style per budget per seed = query cost. Give a parametric formula.\n\nLet me compute a full cost model table.\n\nCost primitives (target-model queries; exclude attacker-LLM queries for PAIR which are separate):\n- AutoDAN 1x = 4800/behavior (120 gen × 40 pop). \n- PAIR 1x = 400/behavior (20 streams × 20 iters).\nRatio 12x. Big asymmetry — motivates split-plot.\n\nTier A: optimize once per (behavior, style, exposure, seed) at 4x, evaluate frozen artifact under all 8 defense cells (2 alignment × 4 inference types).\nArtifacts: N_b behaviors × 2 styles × 3 exposures × S seeds. But exposure level \"publicized\" = library items, not optimized → cheaper. Note.\n\nQueries for Tier A generation: per artifact at 4x: AutoDAN 19,200; PAIR 1,600.\nBut artifacts generated under defense=none (reference). Then Tier A evaluation: each artifact replayed under 8 defense cells but with adaptive-free frozen replay = 1 forward pass per (artifact × defense cell) → cheap: 100% of cost is generation.\n\nActually more precisely: Tier A evaluation cost = n_artifacts × 8 defense cells × ~1-3 queries (replay, maybe k samples for sampling temperature). Say ×3 samples → small.\n\nTier B: full adaptive optimization against each defense → cost multiplies by number of defense cells that require adaptive runs. Select subset: 4 of 8 cells (the ones where adaptivity matters most: none, filter, moderation, strengthened+filter? researcher wants primary style×defense×budget identified).\n\nLet me build a parametric cost function and 3 regimes.\n\nLet me compute numbers.\n\nRegime \"standard\": N_b=100 behaviors (2 datasets × 50? or 100 total), S=2 seeds.\nTier A artifacts per style: 100 behaviors × 3 exposures × 2 seeds = 600 artifacts. AutoDAN: 600 × 19,200 = 11.52M queries. PAIR: 600 × 1,600 = 0.96M. Total ~12.5M target queries. Hmm that's huge but AutoDAN evaluation queries are cheap per token; still 12.5M forwards is too much for \"standard\".\n\nSo need to reduce: use budget checkpoint ladder from a single 4x run (savings 1.31x). Use fewer behaviors for AutoDAN's 4x tier; or reduce AutoDAN population.\n\nAlternative: define 1x AutoDAN = 4800 and cap 4x only for a subset. Let me instead make the ladder: Tier A uses 1x only for full behavior set, plus a budget-curve subset N_b' behaviors × 3 budgets.\n\nLet me restructure:\n- Grid core (budget=1x): all behaviors × styles × exposures × seeds × defense cells (frozen replay).\n- Budget curve (0.25x,1x,4x): on a random subset N_bc behaviors, using checkpointed runs (cost = 4x once).\n\nCost = generation + replay.\n\nLet me compute regimes numerically with a script. Also compute judge calls = number of scored outputs.\n\nJudge calls: Tier A: n_artifacts × 8 cells × samples. Tier B: per-iteration scoring (PAIR uses judge in-loop; AutoDAN uses judge as fitness → every candidate scored!). AutoDAN fitness function is typically keyword/refusal-based, not full LLM judge. Note that: fitness proxy vs final judge must be separated. Good design point: in-loop scoring uses a cheap proxy (keyword/refusal matcher, or a small judge), and final scoring uses the ensemble. This matters for cost.\n\nLet me write the cost script.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cat << 'EOF' > /tmp/model.py\nimport math\n# ---- primitives (assumed canonical; labeled assumptions in design) ----\nQA1, QP1 = 120*40, 20*20           # queries/behavior at 1x\nprint(f\"1x per-behavior target queries: AutoDAN={QA1}  PAIR={QP1}  ratio={QA1/QP1:.0f}x\\n\")\n\ndef tierA_cost(Nb, S, n_exp_opt=2, budget_mult=1.0, n_bc=0, bc_mult=4.0):\n    \"\"\"Generation cost. Frozen artifact replay is ~free by comparison.\n       n_exp_opt: exposures requiring in-study optimization (novel, previously-tested);\n                  'publicized' reuses a frozen library -> no optimization.\"\"\"\n    gen = 0.0\n    for Q1,name in [(QA1,'AutoDAN'),(QP1,'PAIR')]:\n        core = Nb*S*n_exp_opt*Q1*budget_mult\n        bc   = n_bc*S*n_exp_opt*Q1*bc_mult          # checkpointed ladder: pay 4x once\n        gen += core+bc\n    return gen\ndef replay_cost(Nart, n_cells, samples): return Nart*n_cells*samples\n\ndef regime(name, Nb, n_bc, S, Nbc_cells=8, samples=3, tierB_cells=0, tierB_Nb=0, tierB_bud=(1.0,)):\n    gen = tierA_cost(Nb,S,n_bc)\n    Nart = Nb*S*3                     # all 3 exposures have artifacts to replay\n    rep  = replay_cost(Nart, Nbc_cells, samples)\n    tb = 0.0\n    for Q1 in (QA1,QP1):\n        tb += tierB_Nb*S*2*sum(Q1*b for b in tierB_bud)*tierB_cells   # adaptive: re-optimize per cell\n    tot = gen+rep+tb\n    print(f\"{name:9s} Nb={Nb:4d} budget-curve behaviors={n_bc:3d} seeds={S} \"\n          f\"TierB cells={tierB_cells} Nb={tierB_Nb} bud={tierB_bud}\")\n    print(f\"          gen={gen/1e6:8.2f}M  replay={rep/1e6:7.3f}M  TierB={tb/1e6:8.2f}M  TOTAL={tot/1e6:9.2f}M target queries\")\n    print(f\"          attempts scored (primary grid) = {Nart*Nbc_cells*samples}, judge calls @2-family = {2*Nart*Nbc_cells*samples}\\n\")\n    return tot\n\nregime(\"full\",     200, 60, 3, 8, 3, 4, 60, (1.0,4.0))\nregime(\"standard\", 100, 30, 2, 8, 2, 2, 30, (1.0,))\nregime(\"stripped\",  60, 15, 1, 4, 1, 0,  0, ())\n\n# ---- Tier B sizing as a function of a supplied ceiling C (target queries) ----\nprint(\"Tier B feasible behaviors N_B as fn of remaining ceiling C (2 styles, 2 seeds, exposures=2):\")\nprint(\"  N_B = C / [ sum_style Q1_style * sum(budgets) * n_seeds * n_exp * n_cells ]\")\nfor C in [1e6,5e6,2e7]:\n    for cells,buds in [(2,(1.0,)),(4,(1.0,4.0))]:\n        denom = (QA1+QP1)*sum(buds)*2*2*cells\n        print(f\"   C={C/1e6:5.1f}M  cells={cells} budgets={buds} -> N_B = {C/denom:6.1f}\")\nEOF\npython3 /tmp/model.py", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bd50ef7a8ebfd57c", "tool_cal